[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router6’s Linux firewall (iptables) on the Location B gateway. The forwarding path from the WAN/core (eth2: 10.50.1.2) toward the Location B LANs (eth1: 172.18.51.254 and eth0: 172.18.50.254) is being filtered.

Mechanism: Location B hosts (e.g., 172.18.51.15) can reach their default gateway and each other, but return traffic from Location A/C arrives at router6 on eth2 and is evaluated by the FORWARD chain with -i eth2, where blanket DROP rules discard it. Locally originated traffic from router6 to A/C succeeds (not subject to FORWARD), which matches our tests.

Localization: router6 FORWARD chain contains:
- “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP”
- “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP”
- “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP”
Evidence:
- pc15->pc17 OK (intra-B); pc15->pc5 fails.
- router6 can ping pc5 and pc11 successfully.
- router3->pc15 ping times out (return path into B dropped on router6).
- ip_forward=1 and RIP routing tables are correct on router6, so routing is fine; firewall is the blocker.

Root cause: A recent iptables change on router6 that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2 unintentionally blocked all return and inbound traffic to Location B.

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the DROP rules on router6’s FORWARD chain. At minimum, insert “-A FORWARD -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT” before any drops, and add explicit allows for desired prefixes from eth2 to the B LANs; or flush/disable the blanket “-i eth2 -j DROP” rules if not intended. After correction, B hosts should again reach Locations A and C. 
=== END ===